<item>
            <title>[LU-20859] &apos;lfs find --printf&apos; mishandles flags in field width directives</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20859</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;For various format strings which can be handed to a command like &quot;&lt;tt&gt;lfs find $path --printf $format_string&quot;&lt;/tt&gt;, the handling of directive width parsing is incorrect or inconsistent, either between validate_printf_str() and parse_format_width(), or with GNU find.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example erroneous cases:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;code panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;codeContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;code-java&quot;&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&quot;% -6p&quot;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;code-comment&quot;&gt;// &lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&apos;-&apos;&lt;/span&gt; not detected, &lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&apos;-6&apos;&lt;/span&gt; parsed as negative number, incidentally is treated the same as &lt;span class=&quot;code-keyword&quot;&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; the width was parsed as &lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&apos;1023&apos;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&quot;%+6p&quot;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;code-comment&quot;&gt;// validate_printf_str() prints warning however parse_format_width() accepts it normally
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;code-quote&quot;&gt;&quot;% 6p&quot;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;code-comment&quot;&gt;// validate_printf_str() prints warning however parse_format_width() accepts it normally&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beyond those two cases which definitely should be fixed, there are other inconsistencies with how GNU find handles width parameters:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The # char is not accepted&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The + char is only accepted when immediately followed by a digit&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Space with no width digit after (&quot;% p&quot;) is rejected&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Precision, i.e. &quot;%.5p&quot; not implemented (I think leaving this out is fine, doesn&apos;t seem very useful)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;parse_format_width() accepts a greater range of whitespace characters (space, \t, \n, \r, \v, \f) than GNU find (space)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;


&lt;p&gt;I think the simplest solution here is to reject whitespace / +. It doesn&apos;t seem necessary to have full parity with GNU find, as much as a strict implementation of the needed subset.&#160;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
